WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.880
In traditional vector animation, there
is a concept called interpolation curves,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.828
which describes the way we move between
one animation keyframe and the next

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.880
Depending on the shape of this curve,
we can control motion to slowly ease in,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.823
to slowly ease out, or even to
anticipate and overshoot the keyframe

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.257
The choice of animation curves can greatly affect
the feeling of inertia and energy in the motion

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.040
This grants the animator a good deal of
artistic expression in describing motion

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.219
while keeping the number of keyframes economical

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.920
In procedural animation, however, it can be
very powerful to do away with the concept of

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.600
keyframes entirely, freeing the system
to move however the situation demands

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.000
This adaptability is one of the main reasons
to incorporate procedural animation in games

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.960
For example, in the game I’m working on, I have
this little robot character which moves its body

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:03.845
and legs using inverse kinematics in response to
realtime requests to move and change orientation

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.226
I’m controlling it with my mouse here,
and you can find tutorials for how to

00:01:06.226 --> 00:01:10.530
and you can find tutorials for how to
implement this sort of thing online

00:01:11.158 --> 00:01:15.306
In game, this same flexibility allows
the character to immediately respond

00:01:15.306 --> 00:01:18.198
to changing gameplay situations

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.555
This is pretty nice, but because we
don’t have&nbsp;keyframes for these inputs,

00:01:24.555 --> 00:01:28.929
we can't really use interpolation curves
to add that sense of inertia

00:01:28.929 --> 00:01:33.263
What we’d really like is the ability to
achieve something like this,

00:01:33.263 --> 00:01:36.870
using an intuitive&nbsp;abstraction
similar to interpolation curves,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:42.720
but without losing out on the dynamic
responsiveness of a keyframe-free system

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.304
Let’s start off by describing the problem
we want to solve a bit more precisely

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.120
Given some dynamically changing
input x from our game world,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.400
we want to produce some dynamic output y
which tracks the input x, but is imbued with

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.651
some sort of characteristic that conveys
the way that we want the motion to feel

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:11.009
In the simplest case, y=x, and our system
rigidly conforms to whatever input we give it

00:02:11.409 --> 00:02:15.619
This isn’t very useful on its own,
but it is a good starting point

00:02:16.105 --> 00:02:20.581
Whatever changes we make to this equation, we’ll
want to ensure that in the long term,

00:02:20.581 --> 00:02:24.820
when x isn’t changing,
y should eventually settle at x

00:02:25.526 --> 00:02:28.438
To introduce the possibility for interesting dynamics,

00:02:28.438 --> 00:02:32.480
let’s add some velocity and
acceleration terms, represented here as

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.631
first and second order derivatives with respect
to time, scaled by some values k1, k2, and k3

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.640
The dot notation here is just a shorthand
way of writing time derivatives, which

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.117
you may have also seen written like this, or this

00:02:50.061 --> 00:02:53.816
This type of relationship involving
up to a second order time derivative

00:02:53.816 --> 00:02:56.480
is known as a second order system

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.040
Mechanical motions in the real world tend to
be second order, which you can think of as

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.520
being related to the fact that forces act
on acceleration, the second derivative of

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:12.000
position, as described by Newton’s second law
These forces usually arise out of stiffness,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.320
where they are a function of displacement, or
out of friction, where they are a function of

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.205
velocity, hence our choice of terms

00:03:20.105 --> 00:03:23.436
For example, this is the equation of motion of a rigid body

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:27.124
being dragged along&nbsp;one axis through a linear damped spring

00:03:28.309 --> 00:03:32.960
And this is the equation of motion
for the SmoothDamp function in Unity

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.280
By playing around with these values k1, k2, and
k3, we can generate a whole host of different

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.521
interesting behaviors, but as an artist,
it might not be very intuitive

00:03:43.521 --> 00:03:46.865
how these values map to those different behaviors

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:53.435
First, let’s simplify the visualization to look at
the response to a single sudden change in the input

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.240
The resulting output is known as the
step response, which is a standard way

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.430
of visualizing the dynamics of a system, and
captures the essence of the system’s behavior

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.803
Next, using k1 k2 and k3, we’ll
define 3 new variables: f, zeta, and r

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.223
We’ll use f, zeta, and r
(which I’ll&nbsp;explain in a moment)

00:04:16.223 --> 00:04:20.423
as the design parameters for the system’s motion

00:04:20.423 --> 00:04:24.850
Under the hood, we can use them
to compute k1, k2, and k3

00:04:26.292 --> 00:04:31.056
In this new parameterization of f, zeta, and r,
each term controls something

00:04:31.056 --> 00:04:34.409
that&nbsp;we can develop some reasonable intuition about

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.520
f is the natural frequency of the system
measured in Hz, or cycles per second

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.120
It describes the speed at which the system will
respond to changes in the input, as well as the

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.327
frequency the system will tend to vibrate at, but
does not affect the shape of the resulting motion

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.720
zeta is known as the damping coefficient

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:58.640
It describes how the system
comes to settle at the target

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.641
When zeta is 0, vibration never dies
down, and the system is undamped

00:05:04.369 --> 00:05:08.880
Between values of 0 and 1, the system
is underdamped, and will vibrate more,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.819
or less, depending on the magnitude of zeta

00:05:12.533 --> 00:05:15.549
When zeta is greater than 1, the system will not vibrate,

00:05:15.549 --> 00:05:20.524
and instead slowly settle toward&nbsp;the target x,
depending on the magnitude of zeta

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:27.499
The SmoothDamp function in Unity uses a zeta equal
to exactly 1 which is known as critical damping

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.249
r is a value which controls the
initial response of the system

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:37.759
When r is 0, the system takes time
to begin accelerating from rest

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:42.688
When r is positive, the system
reacts immediately to movement in x

00:05:42.688 --> 00:05:47.025
When r is greater than 1, the
system will overshoot the target

00:05:47.025 --> 00:05:51.126
When r is negative, the system
will anticipate the motion

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.573
When modeling a mechanical connection,
a value of 2 for r is typical

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.320
Now, as artists, we have all we
need to start playing with the

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.261
characteristics of the motion of
our procedurally animated object

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.040
If we want to control the settling behavior
of the system, we can play with zeta

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.000
If we want to control the initial
response of the system, we can play with r

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:19.840
If we want to control how fast or slowly
the system behaves, we can play with f

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:26.080
Coming back to this robot character, you can
see I used an underdamped movement for the

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:33.234
body with zeta of 0.5, and r of -2 to give
it some anticipation before fast movements

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.600
For a bit of extra flavor, I tilt
the body towards the target position,

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:43.280
to further hint at the intent behind the motion
I want the turning to be smooth, so for the body

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:49.520
orientation I use a zeta of 1 and r of 0
I do the same for the head orientation,

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.531
but with a smaller f, to make it lag behind
the body as a sort of secondary motion

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:01.840
The cables are animated with a little trick
that I explained over on twitter a while ago

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.880
Let’s now look at an approach for
solving this equation in real-time,

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.097
so that we can begin using it in practice

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.600
There are many ways of numerically
solving differential equations

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.480
such as our second order one, but a particularly
straightforward one is called Euler’s method

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:24.640
Today, I will present a slight variation
known as the semi-implicit Euler method,

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:31.677
which, for our system, happens to have the same
accuracy as the more complex Verlet integration

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.720
First, we need to allocate some state
variables: position and velocity

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.400
These are our estimates of
y and its first derivative

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:44.354
At timestep 0, they need to be
initialized to some initial values

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.640
Now, when the game is running, we’ll want to
iteratively update these variables each frame

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.257
Let’s call the amount of time
that passes between frames T

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:00.480
First, we update the position estimate by
taking the previous iteration’s position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.089
and adding T times the velocity

00:08:04.089 --> 00:08:08.649
Next, we update the velocity by
taking the previous iteration’s velocity

00:08:08.649 --> 00:08:11.188
and adding T times the acceleration

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.920
To compute this acceleration,
recall our equation of motion

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.809
We can solve for acceleration here

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:24.451
And substitute in our most recent
estimates of position and velocity

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.920
Note that in semi-implicit Euler, we are using
the updated position to compute velocity,

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.794
which is slightly different from the regular Euler
method where we use the previous frame’s position

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:42.320
In the case that the input velocity, x dot, is
unknown, we can also estimate it using historical

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:48.333
measurements, for example, by approximating it
as the average velocity since the previous sample

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.840
This algorithm naturally translates
almost line by line into code

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.988
If we were to implement this in-game, we
would see that it does work as designed

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.800
I hooked up the code to allow for f,
zeta, and r to be changed in real time,

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.801
and the system responds appropriately

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.685
There is one big issue that we’ll run
into after a bit of experimentation though

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.640
Set the resonant frequency f too high relative
to the frame rate, and the system becomes

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:27.401
completely unstable, launching itself to infinity

00:09:27.401 --> 00:09:32.121
While it’s possible to simply keep f low
enough such that this type of glitch is unlikely,

00:09:32.121 --> 00:09:35.832
in a game engine, we would really like to
have a stronger guarantee that something&nbsp;like

00:09:35.832 --> 00:09:39.141
this doesn’t happen in corner cases, such as a lag spike

00:09:39.141 --> 00:09:42.088
causing the time step to be much larger than anticipated

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:46.640
This is a problem that can be solved, but
to understand it properly, we’ll have to

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.006
get a bit more technical with our analysis

00:09:50.006 --> 00:09:55.231
The reason why physics solvers like Euler’s method
can become unstable is that fundamentally,

00:09:55.231 --> 00:10:00.114
they are feedback systems whose outputs
from one iteration, here y and its derivative,

00:10:00.114 --> 00:10:04.345
are fed&nbsp;back into later iterations of the computation

00:10:04.345 --> 00:10:07.920
When the time step between frames is
too large compared to the parameters,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.915
it will start accumulating errors over time

00:10:10.915 --> 00:10:15.000
Beyond a critical threshold, these errors
will start to compound on themselves,

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.040
quickly leading to catastrophic failure

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.720
To compute this threshold, we’ll need to
organize the problem into a more standard form

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.960
What we want to do is write the values
of our state variables at frame n+1

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:30.454
in terms of our state variables at frame n

00:10:30.454 --> 00:10:34.938
We can do this by substituting the first equation
into the second, so now&nbsp;both our equations

00:10:34.938 --> 00:10:38.845
have y and its&nbsp;derivative at frame n on the right-hand side

00:10:38.845 --> 00:10:41.929
Now let’s expand this and collect like terms

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.070
This is a system of linear equations,
which we can write in matrix notation

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.127
The system, written this way, is known
as the state-space representation

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.760
This matrix A, known as the
state transition matrix,

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:00.767
describes how each iteration of the state
variables influence the next iteration

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:04.560
Intuitively, you can imagine
that the feedback will be stable

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.453
If A doesn’t cause the state variables to grow

00:11:08.224 --> 00:11:10.728
if A was a number instead of a matrix,

00:11:10.728 --> 00:11:14.480
it would be easier to reason about
the effect that it has on the system

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:20.000
For example, in this simpler system with only
a single state variable and no external forces,

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.236
each iteration is equal to the
previous iteration scaled by A

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.520
When the magnitude of A is less than
1, the magnitude of y at each iteration

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:35.000
will be smaller than the previous iteration,
causing the system to stabilize over time

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.240
When the magnitude of A is greater than 1,
the magnitude of y at each iteration will

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:45.520
be larger than the previous iteration, so
the system will quickly grow beyond control

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:48.436
This is what we’d like to avoid

00:11:49.211 --> 00:11:52.855
In our system, A is a 2x2 matrix, which doesn’t have

00:11:52.855 --> 00:11:55.712
a magnitude&nbsp;that can directly be compared to 1

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:01.410
Instead, it has what we can think of as
two separate magnitudes, called eigenvalues

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:07.760
We won’t get into it here, but suffice to
say that both of these eigenvalues z1 and z2,

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:13.200
which are computed by finding the two solutions to
this equation, must have a magnitude less than 1

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:16.086
for the system to settle over time

00:12:16.979 --> 00:12:19.074
This is computed like this

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.872
Which expands to this quadratic polynomial of z

00:12:24.216 --> 00:12:27.058
Which we can solve using the quadratic formula

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:34.093
Our system will be stable when the
magnitude of this expression is less than 1

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:37.600
Solving this inequality gives
us the following constraint,

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:44.341
that the time step T must be less than the square
root of 4 times k2 plus k1 squared, subtract k1

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:48.732
In our code, we can compute this
maximum stable&nbsp;time step, T critical,

00:12:48.732 --> 00:12:52.358
and when running in-game, compare the time step against it

00:12:52.358 --> 00:12:54.000
If the time step is too large,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:58.044
we can divide it up into smaller steps
that are below the stability threshold

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:02.768
Taking smaller steps requires
performing more computations per frame

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:07.992
To avoid this, we might be able to get
away with slowing down the dynamics instead

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:14.847
In this case, rather than constraining
T, we can solve for k2 and constrain that

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:22.000
Clamping k2 to be above this value is not
physically correct, but recall that our goal

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:28.877
here was just to prevent catastrophic failure,
not to produce an accurate physics simulation

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.960
There are, of course, much more esoteric
places you can take this analysis,

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:39.040
and in a previous draft of this
video, I did go a bit further

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:43.200
For example, this frame-to-frame jittering
behavior that sneaks in when the frequency

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:48.880
is sufficiently high, is caused by negative
eigenvalues, so if we also take that into account

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:54.000
during our analysis, we can impose additional
restrictions to ensure it doesn’t happen

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:01.040
In my own implementation, in case accuracy
is needed, I opt to use a method known as

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:06.910
pole-zero matching to compute values for
k1 and k2 per-frame based on the time step

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:11.360
This produces generally more accurate
results for very fast movement,

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:17.357
but comes with this additional computation
cost that might not suit every application

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:22.287
The idea behind using simple
parametric&nbsp;motion models

00:14:22.287 --> 00:14:27.330
like our f zeta r one extends
some interesting possibilities

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:30.759
Just as an example, we could modulate
the values of these parameters

00:14:30.759 --> 00:14:34.920
to convey gameplay&nbsp;information, so
characters could move with different parameters

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:38.815
depending on their state&nbsp;of awareness,
health, status effects, and so on

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.640
This would help communicate the
situation to players through motion

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:45.578
in a way that’s cheap and easy to iterate on

00:14:46.421 --> 00:14:50.075
In any case, I’ve spent long enough
working on this video instead of my game,

00:14:50.075 --> 00:14:50.575
so

00:14:50.589 --> 00:14:53.859
hopefully&nbsp;this has been an approachable
but substantial introduction to

00:14:53.859 --> 00:14:57.966
some interesting concepts, or&nbsp;maybe
a presentation of some applications of

00:14:57.966 --> 00:15:01.811
mathematics in an unusual but interesting context

00:15:03.121 --> 00:15:06.150
As always, thanks for watching

